NAME
       scanf, sscanf, fscanf - formatted input

SYNOPSIS
       #include <stdio.h>

       int scanf(const char *fmt, ...);
       int sscanf(const char *s, const char *fmt, ...);
       int fscanf(FILE *f, const char *fmt, ...);

DESCRIPTION
       Reads input under the control of FMT and stores the results
       through the pointer arguments.

       Conversions: %d %i %u %x %o for integers, %f and %lf for
       floating point, %s for a whitespace-delimited word, %c for a
       single character, and %% for a literal per cent. A width may be
       given, and * suppresses assignment.

       Whitespace in the format matches any run of whitespace; any
       other character must match exactly.

RETURN VALUE
       The number of items assigned, which may be fewer than requested,
       or EOF when input ended before the first conversion.

NOTES
       %s has no bound unless a width is given, so always write %31s
       rather than %s into a 32-byte buffer.

       vsscanf(const char *s, const char *fmt, va_list ap) takes an
       already-started argument list.

SEE ALSO
       printf(3), strtol(3), getline(3)
